00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
Film Courage: How do you help writers&nbsp;
find the emotional spine of their story?

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.600
Pat Verducci, Writing Coach, Teacher and Mentor:&nbsp;
That's a great question. The emotional spine is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:14.640
the most important thing. It is the most important&nbsp;
thing and oftentimes it's one of the last things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.360
that becomes really clear because sometimes you&nbsp;
have to write through a bunch of stuff to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:26.240
realize what the emotional spine is of your story&nbsp;
but one way that I work with writers on finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:33.920
their the emotional spine of their story is by&nbsp;
using the hero's journey and the reason I actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:40.080
like that story model is I think it's the deepest&nbsp;
kind of psychological model and it really is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:49.120
about fear and facing whatever darkness we have in&nbsp;
ourselves you know in pursuing what we want and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.880
what I do with writers when they're you know&nbsp;
sometimes writers have a really great story but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.280
I'm like what do they want what's the emotional&nbsp;
growth like what's their flaw what's their problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:07.280
we actually do kind of talk through this idea of&nbsp;
the hero's journey and often what's missing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.800
like a fear like some people write&nbsp;
